WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.366
The old wireless oven probe is still in the kitchen drawer.

2
00:00:06.366 --> 00:00:09.137
You push it into the roast, and it calls you.

3
00:00:09.137 --> 00:00:17.943
Its library has a listener: you register a function, and the probe calls it on its own thread, with every new reading, until you unregister it.

4
00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:23.430
A flow is the opposite: it waits to be collected, and hands values over by suspending.

5
00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:28.665
So the bridge needs a little care, and here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

6
00:00:28.665 --> 00:00:31.807
Once I've started collecting, I'm listening, right?

7
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:37.715
Here's a first bridge, built with callbackFlow, a flow builder for listener APIs.

8
00:00:37.715 --> 00:00:39.672
Its block registers a listener.

9
00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:41.693
Each event goes in with trySend.

10
00:00:41.693 --> 00:00:44.156
And awaitClose waits, then unregisters.

11
00:00:44.156 --> 00:00:49.965
The caller launches the collector, gives way once with yield, and the probe fires a reading.

12
00:00:49.965 --> 00:00:51.733
What does the collector get?

13
00:00:54.733 --> 00:00:55.399
Nothing.

14
00:00:55.399 --> 00:00:58.895
The probe printed: fired with listeners=0.

15
00:00:58.895 --> 00:01:01.559
And only after that: registered.

16
00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:03.573
Here's what happened.

17
00:01:03.573 --> 00:01:12.591
collect on a callbackFlow starts a producer coroutine, and your block, with its register call, runs only when that coroutine first gets its turn.

18
00:01:12.591 --> 00:01:16.759
launch and one yield got the collector going, but not its producer.

19
00:01:16.759 --> 00:01:19.744
The reading found no listener, and went nowhere.

20
00:01:19.933 --> 00:01:22.993
So in Kotlin, collecting is not registering.

21
00:01:22.993 --> 00:01:32.173
Reactor's Flux.create in Java runs its lambda inside subscribe, on the calling thread, so the Java version gets the reading at once.

22
00:01:32.173 --> 00:01:37.319
A test must wait for the registration, and real code must not count on it.

23
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.883
Now picture the bridge as the hatch with a shelf from lesson 3, fitted to the probe.

24
00:01:42.883 --> 00:01:47.650
The probe's thread puts each reading on the shelf with trySend, which never waits.

25
00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:53.231
send would wait for room, so it's a suspend function, and a plain listener method can't call it.

26
00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:59.364
awaitClose is you standing at the hatch until you're told to leave, and then taking the hatch down.

27
00:01:59.364 --> 00:02:06.171
It runs whatever ended the flow: take of 2, a cancelled collector, an error from the probe, or the probe closing.

28
00:02:06.333 --> 00:02:09.254
Forget it, and the library sounds an alarm.

29
00:02:09.254 --> 00:02:14.689
Collecting throws an IllegalStateException that names the exact line you forgot.

30
00:02:14.689 --> 00:02:22.908
Reactor has no such check: a Flux.create without onDispose still has its listener after take of 1, and nothing tells you.

31
00:02:23.067 --> 00:02:27.370
It's the rare error message that hands you the fix, word for word.

32
00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:33.107
So the rule: collecting is not registering, and every callbackFlow ends with awaitClose.

33
00:02:33.867 --> 00:02:36.438
Next, the probe's first act on connect.

34
00:02:36.438 --> 00:02:44.351
It replays everything it stored: 200 readings in one burst, from its own thread, while the collector waits at the hatch.

35
00:02:44.351 --> 00:02:45.867
The shelf has 64 spots.

36
00:02:45.867 --> 00:02:47.845
How many readings get through?

37
00:02:50.867 --> 00:02:51.094
65.

38
00:02:51.094 --> 00:02:59.728
64 onto the shelf, and one handed straight to the collector who was already waiting, before the shelf was touched.

39
00:02:59.728 --> 00:03:02.379
The other 135 trySend calls failed.

40
00:03:02.379 --> 00:03:07.832
And trySend fails quietly: it returns a result, and nobody looked at it.

41
00:03:08.533 --> 00:03:10.124
So choose on purpose.

42
00:03:10.124 --> 00:03:17.470
A buffer of 500, right after callbackFlow, snaps onto its shelf, and all 500 stored readings fit.

43
00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:21.181
conflate keeps the first and the last: 0 and 199.

44
00:03:21.181 --> 00:03:27.845
And trySendBlocking looks safe, but it blocks the probe's own thread until there's room.

45
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.364
A failed trySend is a lost reading.

46
00:03:30.364 --> 00:03:33.944
Size the shelf for the burst, or choose what to drop.

47
00:03:34.667 --> 00:03:43.353
Next door, the neighbour's kitchen runs on Reactor, a line of stations where each one asks the one before it for n items.

48
00:03:43.353 --> 00:03:44.933
The bridges translate.

49
00:03:44.933 --> 00:03:48.235
asFlow orders 64, then 64 more after every 64.

50
00:03:48.235 --> 00:03:55.557
asFlux starts the flow at the first request, and when the demand runs out, the flow waits inside emit.

51
00:03:55.733 --> 00:04:02.632
Java can't call a Flow's collect, so give Java a Flux, as the lab's ProbeApi does, or a future.

52
00:04:02.632 --> 00:04:03.576
Never a Flow.

53
00:04:04.267 --> 00:04:05.763
Last, the rehearsal.

54
00:04:05.763 --> 00:04:12.647
runTest puts dinner on a fake clock, virtual time, that jumps from one due task to the next.

55
00:04:12.647 --> 00:04:18.559
With tasks due at 100 and 200 milliseconds, what does advanceTimeBy of 100 run?

56
00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:22.238
Nothing.

57
00:04:22.238 --> 00:04:24.233
It stops just before 100.

58
00:04:24.233 --> 00:04:27.425
The clock reads 100, and fired is empty.

59
00:04:27.425 --> 00:04:31.653
runCurrent then runs what's due now: the task at 100.

60
00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:35.125
And runCurrent is also the fix for our lost reading.

61
00:04:35.125 --> 00:04:41.136
In a test, launch the collector, call runCurrent so the hatch gets fitted, and only then fire.

62
00:04:41.867 --> 00:04:45.033
So, once you start collecting, are you listening?

63
00:04:45.033 --> 00:04:45.550
Not yet.

64
00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:48.717
The hatch is fitted when the producer first runs.

65
00:04:48.717 --> 00:04:55.632
trySend fills a shelf you size for the burst, and awaitClose takes the hatch down, whatever ended the flow.

66
00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:56.536
Quick check.

67
00:04:56.536 --> 00:04:59.973
The collector is cancelled while it waits for a reading.

68
00:04:59.973 --> 00:05:01.815
Which line of the bridge runs?

69
00:05:04.867 --> 00:05:09.887
awaitClose's block: it unregisters the listener, so the probe stops calling.
